= Preface
Throughout the last decades, Walter Rudin's _Principles of Mathematical Analysis_ has become the standard when it comes to Analysis.
I see too many Analysis books seeking to prove with dire speed propositions while others avoid true rigor under the pretence of "being pedagogical".

More than over ninety years ago, Gödel made us aware of the incompletness of mathematics and showed that there are more true statements than proofs and more false propositions than counter-arguments.

The tendency still remains ---however--- to value proof over meaning or interpretation. 
The emergence of _Artificial Intelligence_ (AI) and AI-based-proofs have disencouraged mathematicians and made some believe that mathematics has lost its meaning.

We are mourning over the death of a corpse which we have been carrying for decades: the seek for proof.
The value of a mathematician in modern academia was to prove and propose, answers were always more valuable than the questions.
I say otherwise: there are more questions that answers, and our ultimate way to understand is to inquire.


